\item[* name:] TNoPROstatus
\item[* relevant examples:] x2 prevent this ( we talked about how we could 
prevent this)
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] CLAUSEtoSENTENCE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_ProStatus
\item[* date of creation:] 20-APR-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] TPROstatus
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To make `closed' clauses
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) This rule 
functions as the alternative to the TPROstatus rule, which makes open clauses. 
In analysis, any open clause will have to go through that rule, so that only 
closed clauses enter the XPPROPtoCLAUSE subgrammar. In generation, there is a 
free choice for all infinite sentences to pick either this rule or the 
following.
